  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Homework 3: Second rank never answers, first rank is fine"
            prompt="This board has two DDR chips populated as two separate ranks. The first rank's memory test runs clean. The second rank's test never gets a single word back, as if it isn't populated at all - but it physically is, and both chips show the same CK/command activity on a scope. What's wrong?"
            hint="Check each populated chip's own Chip number setting - do the two chips actually disagree with each other, the way two separate ranks should?"
            explanation="Both chips are strapped Chip number = 0 - a board assembly mistake, not a missing or dead part: the second chip's CS strap was never actually changed from the first rank's wiring. When the controller addresses &quot;chip 1&quot; to test the second rank, nothing on the board answers CS1, because nothing is actually wired to it - both chips are listening on CS0 instead. This is a different root cause from the single-chip &quot;wrong target&quot; homework (DDR Homework 1) even though the symptom looks identical: a populated-but-silent rank means check chip-select strapping on every populated chip, not just assume the one you can't reach is faulty or unpowered."/>
</LABCHAIN>
